mkfifo
Usage: mkfifo [-m MODE] NAME

Create a named pipe: a file two programs talk through.

A `|` pipe joins two commands on one line. A named pipe is the same
channel with a name in the filesystem, so unrelated programs, started at
different times, can meet at it. What one writes goes straight to
whoever is reading; nothing lands on disk. A write waits until a reader
shows up, and a read waits until something is written.

	-m MODE	Permissions, octal (600) or symbolic (u=rw). Default a=rw

	mkfifo /tmp/p
	tail -f /tmp/p &
	echo hello > /tmp/p

The irc client on this system is built out of these: ii gives each
channel a directory with an `in` fifo, and you talk by echoing a line
into a file.

A fifo anyone can write to is a fifo anyone can feed, so `-m 600` is the
one you want unless you meant to be reachable; see `chmod` for what the
digits add up to.

See also: chmod, ii, tail, tee
